rdumtool - RDTech UM24C/UM25C/UM34C Bluetooth interface tool

This script is currently in an early stage and could change
significantly.

The RDTech (RuiDeng) UM24C, UM25C and UM34C are low-cost USB
pass-through power measurement devices, and support a decent number of
collection features, as well as full control via Bluetooth. (The non-C
versions of these devices support the same features as the C versions,
but without Bluetooth control.) This program implements all exposed
commands and data collection available by the device’s Bluetooth
interface.

Compatibility

- UM24C support is complete and tested.
- UM25C and UM34C support should be complete, but has not been tested.
  Hardware should arrive by March 2019.
- Requires Python 3.10 or later. Automated tests are configured for
  Python 3.10–3.14; actual support depends on test results and optional
  transport dependencies.
- Linux: Tested fine with both PyBluez (direct) and pyserial
  (e.g. /dev/rfcomm0 via rfcomm bind).
- Windows: Tested fine with pyserial (e.g. COM4 as set up automatically
  by Windows). Author could not get PyBluez compiled/installed.
- MacOS: When using pyserial (e.g. /dev/cu.UM24C-Port as set up
  automatically by MacOS), writes to the device would succeed (e.g. 0xf2
  to rotate the screen), but reads from the device never arrive. Author
  could not get PyBluez compiled/installed.

Setup

rdumtool requires Python 3.10 or later. On Linux, a known Bluetooth
address can be used via Python’s built-in RFCOMM socket support, without
PyBluez, provided the Python build includes Bluetooth socket support.
Automatic Bluetooth discovery requires PyBluez, and serial connections
require pyserial. PyBluez installation and compatibility vary by
operating system and Python version.

To install rdumtool:

    $ python3 -m pip install .

For serial support, install with python3 -m pip install '.[serial]'. For
development, use python3 -m pip install -e '.[serial]'. In an externally
managed Python installation, use a virtual environment rather than sudo.
rdumtool may also be run directly from its source directory with
python3 -m rdum.

Run the automated tests with make test and build a wheel with
make build.

Bluetooth setup

Varies by operating system. If the pairing procedure asks for a PIN,
enter “1234” verbatim.

For command-line installation on Linux:

    $ bluetoothctl
    Agent registered
    [bluetooth]# scan on
    Discovery started
    [NEW] Device 00:90:72:56:98:D7 UM24C
    [CHG] Device 00:90:72:56:98:D7 RSSI: -60
    [bluetooth]# pair 00:90:72:56:98:D7
    Attempting to pair with 00:90:72:56:98:D7
    [CHG] Device 00:90:72:56:98:D7 Connected: yes
    Request PIN code
    [UM241m[agent] Enter PIN code: 1234
    [CHG] Device 00:90:72:56:98:D7 UUIDs: 00001101-0000-1000-8000-00805f9b34fb
    [CHG] Device 00:90:72:56:98:D7 ServicesResolved: yes
    [CHG] Device 00:90:72:56:98:D7 Paired: yes
    Pairing successful
    [bluetooth]# trust 00:90:72:56:98:D7
    [CHG] Device 00:90:72:56:98:D7 Trusted: yes
    Changing 00:90:72:56:98:D7 trust succeeded
    [bluetooth]# exit
    Agent unregistered

Device MAC address will vary. Again, the PIN for the device is “1234”.

If you then want to use rdumtool via pyserial, bind it via rfcomm:

    $ sudo rfcomm bind 0 00:90:72:56:98:D7

Usage

To get device information via PyBluez:

    $ rdumtool --device-type=UM24C --bluetooth-device=00:90:72:56:98:D7

Or via pyserial:

    $ rdumtool --device-type=UM24C --serial-device=/dev/rfcomm0

Without any additional arguments, rdumtool will display all information
available from the device. There are various additional flags to set
parameters in the device such as threshold logging; see rdumtool --help
for details.

Some meters advertise under a different name, such as E-test. In that
case, specify the Bluetooth address explicitly instead of relying on
automatic discovery. Select the actual model with --device-type: UM24C
and UM25C packets share markers but use different voltage and current
scales, so the model cannot be identified from the packet markers alone.
Reads time out after five seconds rather than waiting indefinitely.

Firmware and protocol notes

rdumtool communicates with the meter; it does not read program memory,
update firmware, or report a firmware build/version. The 130-byte status
response has no firmware-version field, so this interface cannot recover
a firmware image or identify its exact revision.

The sigrok reverse-engineering notes report that the manufacturer does
not provide firmware updates and that the firmware is not designed to be
upgradeable. They identify an STM8 microcontroller on the main board and
exposed SWIM debugging pins, but whether the chip can be dumped is
unclear. The same notes identify a Beken BK3231 Bluetooth module on the
UM24C and a DX-BT18 module on the UM25C/UM34C; these are
Bluetooth-module details, not firmware version identifiers. These
hardware observations are from the referenced notes and may not cover
every board revision.

The C models expose a serial-over-Bluetooth interface. The protocol is
reverse-engineered rather than manufacturer-documented: serial settings
are 9600 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, and one stop bit. The host sends
one-byte commands; 0xf0 requests a 130-byte status response, while
control/configuration commands do not acknowledge. rdumtool uses a
five-second read timeout and waits 0.5 seconds between configured
commands.

Commands

- 0xf0 (all models): Request status frame.
- 0xf1 (all models): Next screen.
- 0xf2 (all models): Rotate screen.
- 0xf3: Next data group on UM24C; previous screen on UM25C/UM34C.
- 0xf4 (all models): Clear current data group.
- 0xa0–0xa9 (UM25C/UM34C): Select data group 0–9.
- 0xb0–0xce (all models): Set recording threshold 0.00–0.30 A in 0.01 A
  steps.
- 0xd0–0xd5 (all models): Set brightness 0–5.
- 0xe0–0xe9 (all models): Set screen timeout 0–9 minutes; 0 disables it.

Status-frame layout

Offsets are zero-based. Multi-byte values are unsigned, big-endian. The
frame starts with a model marker and ends with a model-specific marker:
UM24C and UM25C use 09 63 / ff f1; UM34C uses 0d 4c / 80 68. These
markers do not distinguish UM24C from UM25C.

- Bytes 0–1: Start marker.
- Bytes 2–3: Voltage (UM25C: mV; UM24C/UM34C: 0.01 V).
- Bytes 4–5: Current (UM25C: 0.1 mA; UM24C/UM34C: mA).
- Bytes 6–9: Power (mW).
- Bytes 10–11: Temperature in Celsius.
- Bytes 12–13: Temperature in Fahrenheit.
- Bytes 14–15: Selected data group (zero-based index).
- Bytes 16–95: Ten data-group records, each 8 bytes: 4-byte mAh followed
  by 4-byte mWh.
- Bytes 96–97: Positive data-line voltage (0.01 V).
- Bytes 98–99: Negative data-line voltage (0.01 V).
- Bytes 100–101: Charging-mode code (see below).
- Bytes 102–105: Threshold-recorded capacity (mAh).
- Bytes 106–109: Threshold-recorded energy (mWh).
- Bytes 110–111: Recording threshold (0.01 A).
- Bytes 112–115: Recording duration (seconds).
- Bytes 116–117: Recording state (zero is off; nonzero is on).
- Bytes 118–119: Screen timeout in minutes (zero means disabled).
- Bytes 120–121: Screen brightness (level 0–5).
- Bytes 122–125: Resistance (0.1 ohm).
- Bytes 126–127: Selected screen index.
- Bytes 128–129: End marker.

Charging-mode codes interpreted by rdumtool are 0 unknown/normal, 1
Quick Charge 2.0, 2 Quick Charge 3.0, 3 Apple 2.4 A, 4 Apple 2.1 A, 5
Apple 1.0 A, 6 Apple 0.5 A, 7 DCP 1.5 A, and 8 Samsung. Detection
capabilities vary by model; the meter’s reported mode is not itself a
firmware identifier.

This packet layout and command map are reverse-engineered and are
implemented in rdumtool, but UM25C and UM34C behavior has not been
verified on hardware. Treat model-specific compatibility as provisional.

Example

    $ rdumtool --device-type=UM24C --bluetooth-device=00:90:72:56:98:D7 --rotate-screen --set-record-threshold=0.20

    $ rdumtool --device-type=UM24C --bluetooth-device=00:90:72:56:98:D7
    rdumtool 1.0
    Copyright (C) 2019 Ryan Finnie

    USB:  5.08V,  0.166A,  0.843W,   30.6Ω
    Data:  2.99V(+),  0.00V(-), charging mode: unknown (normal)
    Recording (on) :    0.009Ah,    0.046Wh,  197 sec at >= 0.20A
    Data groups:
         0:    0.015Ah,    0.077Wh       5:    0.000Ah,    0.000Wh
         1:    0.000Ah,    0.000Wh       6:    0.000Ah,    0.000Wh
        *2:    0.040Ah,    0.204Wh       7:    0.000Ah,    0.000Wh
         3:    0.000Ah,    0.000Wh       8:    0.000Ah,    0.000Wh
         4:    0.000Ah,    0.000Wh       9:    0.000Ah,    0.000Wh
    Temp:  25C ( 77F)
    Screen: 1/6, brightness: 5/5, timeout: 2 min

Miscellaneous

Thanks to the sigrok wiki for a reverse engineering of the UM24C’s
communication protocol, which was referenced to produce this tool.

Thanks to RDTech for their power management devices. The author
currently has a UM24C, and a DPS3003 DC-DC power supply, both of which
are excellent quality and performance for their price. Even more thanks
would be in order if they released proper protocol specifications and
design schematics, hint hint.
